## The core idea
Most "state management" pain in big apps comes from treating all state as one kind. Ask two questions about every piece of data: **who owns the truth** (the server, the URL, the user's current interaction) and **who needs to read it** (one component, a subtree, the whole app). Once you classify state, the tool almost picks itself, and the global store shrinks to a small fraction of what teams usually put in it.

## Classify first
| Kind | Example | Source of truth | Good home |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Server cache | user profile, orders list | the backend | TanStack Query, SWR, RTK Query, RSC |
| URL state | filters, page number, selected tab | the address bar | `searchParams`, router |
| Form state | draft values, validation errors | the form while editing | React Hook Form, `useActionState`, local state |
| Local UI state | is this dropdown open | one component | `useState` |
| Shared client state | theme, sidebar collapsed, a multi-step wizard, an editor's selection | the client | Context (low churn) or Zustand / Jotai / Redux Toolkit |

Rule of thumb from production codebases: once server data moves into a query cache and filters move into the URL, the remaining global client state is usually small.

## Server state is a cache, not state
Server data has properties your store does not model well: it can go stale, other users change it, many components request the same thing, and you need loading, error, retry, deduplication, pagination and background refetch. TanStack Query and SWR are caches keyed by a query key:

```tsx
function useOrders(status: string) {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: ["orders", { status }],
    queryFn: () => api.orders.list({ status }),
    staleTime: 30_000, // treat as fresh for 30 s, no refetch on remount
  });
}
```

Two components calling `useOrders("open")` share one request and one cache entry. `staleTime` controls when data counts as stale; `gcTime` controls how long unused entries stay in memory. Mutations invalidate keys (`queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ["orders"] })`) instead of hand-editing a global store.

## Optimistic updates
Show the result before the server confirms, then reconcile:

```tsx
const qc = useQueryClient();
const toggle = useMutation({
  mutationFn: (id: string) => api.todos.toggle(id),
  onMutate: async (id) => {
    await qc.cancelQueries({ queryKey: ["todos"] }); // stop in-flight refetch overwriting us
    const previous = qc.getQueryData<Todo[]>(["todos"]);
    qc.setQueryData<Todo[]>(["todos"], (old = []) =>
      old.map((t) => (t.id === id ? { ...t, done: !t.done } : t)),
    );
    return { previous };
  },
  onError: (_err, _id, ctx) => qc.setQueryData(["todos"], ctx?.previous), // roll back
  onSettled: () => qc.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ["todos"] }), // resync with truth
});
```

React 19's `useOptimistic` gives the same idea for Server Actions: an optimistic value that reverts automatically when the action settles. Use optimistic UI for actions that almost always succeed (likes, toggles, reorder); avoid it for payments or anything where showing a false success is harmful.

## Client state libraries: the real trade-offs
- **Context** is dependency injection, not a store. Every consumer re-renders when the value changes, and there is no selector. Fine for theme, locale, current user; bad for fast-changing values.
- **Zustand**: a tiny store with selector subscriptions. Components re-render only when their selected slice changes. Minimal boilerplate, works outside React.
- **Redux Toolkit**: more structure (slices, actions, middleware, devtools time travel), `createEntityAdapter` for normalised collections, RTK Query for server cache. Good when many teams need enforced conventions and auditable state transitions.
- **Jotai**: atomic state; you compose small atoms and derived atoms, and components subscribe to exactly the atoms they read. Good for editor-like apps with many independent pieces.

```ts
const useCart = create<CartState>()((set) => ({
  items: [],
  add: (item) => set((s) => ({ items: [...s.items, item] })),
}));
// Re-renders only when the count changes, not on every cart mutation
const count = useCart((s) => s.items.length);
```

## Normalisation
If the same entity appears in several lists (a user in comments, in a sidebar, in a mention), store entities once by id and keep lists as arrays of ids:

```text
entities: { users: { u1: {...}, u2: {...} }, posts: { p1: { author: "u1" } } }
lists:    { feed: ["p1", "p7"], pinned: ["p7"] }
```
An update to `u1` then updates everywhere at once. Query caches are not normalised by default; you either invalidate broadly or patch several keys. Apollo and Relay normalise GraphQL responses automatically, which is a big reason teams choose them.

## Prop drilling and the context re-render trap
Prop drilling three levels is fine and explicit. When it gets painful, try **composition** first (pass `children` or render props so intermediate components do not need the data), then context for low-churn values, then a store.

The trap: one big context holding `{ user, theme, cart, notifications }` with a new object every render. Every consumer re-renders on any change. Fixes: split contexts by change frequency, memoise the value, separate state and dispatch into two contexts, or move hot values to a store with selectors.

## Common mistakes
- Copying server data into `useState` or Redux and then fighting to keep it in sync.
- Syncing state with `useEffect` (derive it during render instead).
- Keeping filters in component state, so refresh and shared links lose them.
- A global store as a dumping ground, with no ownership per feature.
- Optimistic updates without cancelling in-flight queries, so a refetch flashes the old value.

## In the interview
Lead with classification. Interviewers are testing whether you reach for a global store by reflex. Then show depth on one area: query keys and invalidation, an optimistic rollback, or why context re-renders. Senior answers talk about conventions across teams: one server-cache library, query-key factories per domain, URL as the source of truth for anything shareable, and lint rules against importing another feature's store.

## The core idea
Tests exist to let many people change code confidently. At scale the enemies are not missing tests but **slow, flaky and brittle** tests: suites that take 40 minutes, fail randomly, or break on every refactor. The strategy is to put most effort where tests give the most confidence per second of runtime and per hour of maintenance.

## The testing trophy
Kent C. Dodds' "testing trophy" reshapes the classic pyramid for frontends:

```text
        /  E2E  \          few: critical user journeys in a real browser
      /Integration\        most: components + hooks + state + mocked network
      \   Unit    /        some: pure logic (formatters, reducers, validators)
       \ Static  /         always: TypeScript, ESLint
```
Static analysis catches typos and type errors for free. Unit tests suit pure functions. **Integration tests** (render a feature, interact like a user, assert what the user sees) give the best confidence per cost for UI. E2E tests cover the few journeys that make money or would be catastrophic to break: sign-in, checkout, publish.

## Integration tests with React Testing Library
RTL's principle: "the more your tests resemble the way your software is used, the more confidence they can give you." Query by role and accessible name, not CSS classes or component internals.

```tsx
test("applies a discount code", async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<Checkout />, { wrapper: Providers });

  await user.type(screen.getByRole("textbox", { name: /discount code/i }), "SAVE10");
  await user.click(screen.getByRole("button", { name: /apply/i }));

  expect(await screen.findByText(/10% off applied/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
  expect(screen.getByRole("status")).toHaveTextContent("$90.00");
});
```
Because the test only knows roles and text, you can refactor from Redux to Zustand or split components without touching it. A bonus: querying by role fails when the UI is inaccessible. Vitest has largely replaced Jest in Vite-based stacks; Vitest's browser mode runs component tests in a real browser when jsdom is not faithful enough.

## Mock the network, not your code: MSW
Mock Service Worker intercepts requests at the network level: a Service Worker in the browser, request interception in Node. Your components and data layer run unchanged; only the HTTP responses are fake.

```ts
import { http, HttpResponse } from "msw";
import { setupServer } from "msw/node";

export const handlers = [
  http.get("/api/cart", () => HttpResponse.json({ items: [], total: 0 })),
  http.post("/api/discount", async ({ request }) => {
    const { code } = (await request.json()) as { code: string };
    return code === "SAVE10"
      ? HttpResponse.json({ percent: 10 })
      : HttpResponse.json({ error: "invalid" }, { status: 422 });
  }),
];
export const server = setupServer(...handlers);
// vitest.setup.ts: beforeAll(() => server.listen()); afterEach(() => server.resetHandlers()); afterAll(() => server.close());
```
Override per test with `server.use(...)` for error states. The same handlers power Storybook and local development without a backend.

## E2E with Playwright
Playwright drives Chromium, Firefox and WebKit, auto-waits for elements to be actionable, isolates each test in a fresh browser context, and records traces (DOM snapshots, network, console) for debugging failures.

```ts
test("guest can check out", async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto("/products/blue-mug");
  await page.getByRole("button", { name: "Add to cart" }).click();
  await page.getByRole("link", { name: "Cart (1)" }).click();
  await page.getByRole("button", { name: "Checkout" }).click();
  await expect(page.getByRole("heading", { name: "Order confirmed" })).toBeVisible();
});
```
At scale: seed test data through APIs (not the UI), reuse authenticated state (`storageState`) instead of logging in through the form every test, shard across CI machines, and run a smoke subset on every PR with the full suite on merge.

## Visual regression
Unit and E2E tests miss "the button is now white on white". Visual tests screenshot components or pages and diff against approved baselines: Playwright's `expect(page).toHaveScreenshot()`, or hosted services like Chromatic or Percy on Storybook stories. Keep them stable: fixed viewport, fonts loaded, animations disabled, the same OS and browser in CI (run in Docker), mocked dates and data.

## Contract tests
When frontend and backend deploy separately, mocks can drift from reality: the test passes against a fake response the real API no longer returns. Options:
- **Consumer-driven contracts** (Pact): the frontend records the requests and the response shapes it relies on; the provider's CI verifies it still satisfies them.
- **Schema-driven**: generate types and MSW handlers from the OpenAPI or GraphQL schema, so a breaking schema change fails type-check.
- Validate API responses at runtime with zod at the boundary, and report mismatches to error tracking.

## Accessibility tests in CI
Automated tools catch roughly a third of WCAG issues (missing labels, contrast, invalid ARIA), which is still worth automating:
```ts
import AxeBuilder from "@axe-core/playwright";
const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page }).withTags(["wcag2a", "wcag2aa"]).analyze();
expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);
```
Also `eslint-plugin-jsx-a11y`, the Storybook a11y addon, and RTL role queries. Manual keyboard and screen-reader checks are still needed for flows.

## Flaky tests
A flaky test fails sometimes without a code change. At scale they destroy trust: people re-run CI until it is green, and real failures slip through.
- **Detect**: track pass/fail per test across runs; a test that both passes and fails on the same commit is flaky.
- **Quarantine**: move it out of the blocking suite automatically, file a ticket with an owner, fix or delete within a deadline.
- **Fix root causes**: arbitrary `waitForTimeout` sleeps (use web-first assertions that retry), shared state between tests, order dependence, real time and timezones (fake the clock), animations, real third-party network calls, and test data collisions in parallel runs.
- Retries (`retries: 2` in CI) are a bandage that hides signal; report "passed on retry" as flaky rather than green.

## Common mistakes
- Testing implementation details (state values, internal method calls), so refactors break tests.
- Snapshot tests of huge component trees that everyone updates blindly.
- Too many E2E tests for edge cases that integration tests could cover in milliseconds.
- Mocking the module that fetches data instead of the network, so the data layer is never tested.
- Ignoring flaky tests until the suite is not trusted.

## In the interview
They probe judgement: what you test at which level and why, how you keep a big suite fast and trustworthy, and how you handle flakiness as an engineering process. Name concrete tools (RTL, MSW, Playwright, axe, Chromatic), explain why role-based queries make tests resilient, and give a flaky-test policy with detection, quarantine and ownership.

deepDives.body:
A remote can fail in two ways: its `remoteEntry.js` fails to load (CDN outage, bad deploy, network), or it loads and throws while rendering.

```tsx
function RemoteSlot({ name, load }: { name: string; load: () => Promise<{ default: ComponentType }> }) {
  const Lazy = useMemo(() => lazy(() => withTimeout(load(), 8000)), [load]);
  return (
    <ErrorBoundary fallback={<RemoteUnavailable name={name} />} onError={(e) => report(e, { remote: name })}>
      <Suspense fallback={<RemoteSkeleton />}>
        <Lazy />
      </Suspense>
    </ErrorBoundary>
  );
}
```

- Timeout plus one retry, then a fallback that keeps the rest of the shell usable.
- Report with remote name and version tags so the right team is paged.
- Keep previously deployed versions on the CDN so the manifest can roll back instantly and open tabs keep working.
- Preload the current route's `remoteEntry.js` with a `link rel="modulepreload"` or `preload` hint emitted by the shell to avoid the shell -> manifest -> entry -> chunk waterfall.
